\par\par \b \uc2  Japan  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulation has backfired - bankers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 19 (Reuter) - Japan's enthusiastic attempt to propel the dollar above 90 yen, initiated by bureaucrats at the country's powerful finance ministry, has backfired and may well undermine the dollar's natural rally, senior bankers say. \par \par \uc2  "Efforts to push up the dollar have got to the stage where they may undermine its natural upward correction," said Yukuo Takahashi, Managing Director of Chemical Bank Tokyo Branch, said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar took a dive in Tokyo on Wednesday, trading around 87.60 yen in late afternoon trade after hitting a 3-1/2 month high of 89.27 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Takahashi said the dollar's rebound from a long period of decline had started in mid-April and could have lasted up to six months, if it had been allowed to take its natural course. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) manoeuvre overstimulated market expectations of the dollar's strength, inducing aggressive buying by market participants that could not be sustained by market forces. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's reversal can be undercut if Japan is preoccupied with pushing it up without implementing fundamental structural changes," Takahashi said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed after the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on July 7. The action, coming after Washington and Tokyo eased credit in tandem, persuaded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that they had agreed on the need for a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government sources told Reuters on Tuesday that Japan had set a near-term target of pushing the dollar to around 90 yen and Washington "understands" Tokyo's position. \par \par \uc2  MOF bureaucrats have been busy persuading Japanese institutional investors that the dollar will recover to 90-95 yen in the near term, fund managers said. \par \par \uc2  "I assume Washington took the position of 'not objecting to' Tokyo's idea of pushing the dollar up to above 90 yen because it wanted to ensure scope for credit easing in view of the risks of recession," said Yasuhiko Matsunaga, senior Treasury manager of Industrial Bank of Japan Ltd. \par \par \uc2  But if the U.S. economic recovery proves stronger than expected later in the year, the United States has no reason to lend support to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, he added. \par \par \uc2  To back the MOF's initiative, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) has been persistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying dollars. Market sources said MOF recently gave banks guidance aimed at ensuring they do not speak about client orders, including BOJ ones. \par \par \uc2  "But the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is losing power as market hopes for a dollar recovery to above 90 yen recede," said Yoshimori, Matsushita, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Bank. \par \par \uc2  IBJ's Matsunaga said the U.S. Federal Reserve needed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now to provide evidence of the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiative. Fed dollar-buying would induce more buying by Japanese investors, which would be the only effective force to fight significant selling pressure, he added. \par \par \uc2  U.S. hedge funds, interbank dealers and even institutional investors appear to have bought too many dollars in the recent dollar recovery initiated by MOF, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said they detected the political ambitions of Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura, who heads the small Sakigake party in Japan's coalition, behind the MOF move. \par \par \uc2  Recoveries in the dollar and Tokyo stock prices would help provide political points ahead of Sunday's Upper House elections -- a major test for Prime Minister Tomiichi Murayama's three-party coalition. \par \par \uc2  Japan's main share index, the 225-share Nikkei average, closed down 151.91 points at 16,422.44 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr7j05rmp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
