\par\par \b \uc2  Japan  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  talks tough on dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 19 (Reuter) - Japan's often soft-spoken \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  talked tough on Wednesday, warning \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players they would get burnt if they bet on a weaker dollar. \par \par \uc2  "A temporary correction of the dollar's rise cannot be avoided to some extent, but market operators who try to reverse the recent trend (of a rising dollar) will certainly be disappointed -- and they will get burnt," a senior Bank of Japan (BOJ) official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ will make its utmost efforts to ensure an orderly reversal of the dollar," he added. \par \par \uc2  The comments were unusually forthright for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which more typically confines itself to more cryptic remarks. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ had already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo trade, buying dollars at around 88 yen to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which was being knocked down by selling orders. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ challenge, which was followed by more dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , came as a round of selling ended. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been strengthening after the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on July 7. \par \par \uc2  The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , coming after Washington and Tokyo moved in tandem to ease credit, persuaded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that the two nations had agreed on the need for a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government sources told Reuters on Tuesday that Japan had set a near-term target of pushing the dollar to around 90 yen and would step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed to attain that goal. In fact, the BOJ has been persistently doing just that already. \par \par \uc2  The United States "understands" Tokyo's position, the government sources added. \par \par \uc2  Some sources said a rise in the dollar to only around 90 yen would not be enough to support Japan's troubled economy, but they declined to specify any target beyond that level. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts said the dollar, which rose to a 3-1/2 month high of around 89.20 yen in New York on Monday but fell back on Tuesday, would likely drift lower before mounting another rally. \par \par \uc2  It traded at around 87.68 yen in later morning Tokyo trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr7j05rcc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
