\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  manipulation backfires -- bankers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 19 (Reuter) - Japan's enthusiastic attempt to push the dollar to above 90 yen, initiated by bureaucrats of Tokyo's powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), has backfired and may well undercut the dollar's natural reversal, senior bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar took a dive to 87.30 yen in early Tokyo after hitting a 3-1/2 month high of 89.27 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "Efforts to push up the dollar have got to the stage where they may undermine its natural upward correction," said Yukuo Takahashi, Managing Director of Chemical Bank Tokyo Branch. \par \par \uc2  He said the natural reversal of the dollar had started in mid-April and if it had not been for inflamed market expectations, this could have lasted up to six months. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's reversal can be undercut if Japan is preoccupied with pushing it up without implementing fundamental structural changes," he said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was strengthened after the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on July 7. \par \par \uc2  The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , coming after Washington and Tokyo moved in tandem to ease credit, persuaded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that the two had agreed on the need for a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government sources told Reuters on Tuesday that Japan had set a near-term target of pushing the dollar to around 90 yen and the U.S. "understands" Tokyo's position. \par \par \uc2  MOF bureaucrats have been busy persuading Japanese institutional investors that the dollar will recover to 90-95 yen in the near-term, fund managers said. \par \par \uc2  "I assume Washington took the position of 'not objecting to' Tokyo's idea of pushing the dollar up to above 90 yen because it wanted to ensure scope for credit easing in view of the risks of recession," said Yasuhiko Matsunaga, senior Treasury manager of Industrial Bank of Japan Ltd. \par \par \uc2  But, if the U.S. economic recovery proves stronger than expected later in the year, the United States has no reason to lend support to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, he added. \par \par \uc2  To underwrite the MOF initiative, the BOJ has been persistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said MOF recently gave banks guidance aimed at ensuring they do not speak about client orders, including those from the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "But the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is losing power as market hopes for a dollar recovery to above 90 yen recede," said Yoshimori, Matsushita, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager of Mitsubishi Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now to give evidence of the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiative," said IBJ's Matsunaga. \par \par \uc2  Fed dollar-buying would change the mood and induce more buying by Japanese investors, which would be the only effective force to fight significant selling pressure, he added. \par \par \uc2  U.S. hedge funds, interbank dealers and even institutional investors appeared to have bought too many dollars in the recent dollar recovery initiated by MOF, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a dollar recovery, which could lend support to the shaky rebound by Tokyo stocks, means a lot not only to bureaucrats but also to politicians. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said they detected Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura's political ambitions behind the MOF initiative. \par \par \uc2  Recoveries in the dollar and Tokyo stocks would help provide political points ahead of Sunday's Upper House elections -- a major test for Prime Minister Tomiichi Murayama's ruling three-party coalition. \par \par \uc2  Takemura heads the Sakigake party in the coalition. \par \par \uc2  But in response to the dollar's tumble and declines on Wall Street, Japan's main share index, the 225-share Nikkei average, was down 230.62 points at 16,343.73 at 0522 GMT. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr7j05rc6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
