\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Finance Ministry -3: Conflicting Rumors About BOJ \b0\par\par\uc2 169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1995\par 09:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been rife with rumors recently about continuous buying of dollars against the yen by the Bank of Japan to lift the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward 90 yen. At the same time, however, there are rumors in the market that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at all and that the recent rise by the dollar is part of a natural trend. \par \par \uc2  Approached on these rumors, the finance ministry official declined comment. \par \par \uc2  Several \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants have said over the past week that the Bank of Japan has in fact stepped into the market to purchase dollars, but some observers have speculated that this confirmation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is actually only 'trader talk.' \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading Wednesday around 0705 GMT, the dollar is changing hands at 87.51 yen, well below its levels above 89.00 yen a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 19-07-95 \par \par \uc2  0707GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr7j05e7i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
