\par\par \b \uc2  Bundesbank rate cut would not spark big dollar rally. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 12 (Reuter) - A cut in official German interest rates at Thursday's Bundesbank council meeting would catch many traders off-guard but analysts doubt whether it would spark a significant dollar rally. \par \par \uc2  A cut by Germany so soon after last week's reductions by the United States and Japan would raise speculation the big three were acting in concert and change the market's perception. But problems remain for the dollar, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "If they (the Bundesbank) cut rates it would clearly boost the dollar but I don't think we would see a significant rally," said Nick Stamenkovic, economist at DKB International. \par \par \uc2  A Reuter poll of 20 economists conducted on Tuesday showed that 95 percent of them did not expect the Bundesbank to cut official rates at Thursday's fortnightly council meeting, the last before a four-week summer break. \par \par \uc2  Since the rate cuts and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the beleaguered U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week the dollar has risen around 2.5 yen to 87.60 yen and 2.0 pfennigs to 1.4060 marks. \par \par \uc2  One thing holding the dollar back is a belief that the major industrial nations have no coordinated policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although at past meetings of the Group of Seven rich nations official rhetoric was aimed at convincing the market they have. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said it was this lack of coordination that made past bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  less effective than they might have been. The Bundesbank was notable by its absence last Friday when the Federal Reserve and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If the Bundesbank had come in alongside the U.S. and Japan on Friday things would be different now but it is pretty clear that the Bundesbank's priority is the domestic economy," said one trader at a leading U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  He speculated that the Bundesbank would tend to steer clear of doing anything that might indicate it was acting for anything other than domestic factors, so a rate cut would seem unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "It was significant that the Germans were not involved last Friday which is why you have to wonder why there are some people expecting them to do something significant on Thursday," said Nikki Nelson-Smith, economist at Hambros Bank. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't smack of coordination to me, if they do anything it will be for their own reasons," she added. \par \par \uc2  But even if the Bundesbank does spring a surprise it is not going to ignite bullish fervour for the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  There are still huge twin deficits in the United States and the Federal Reserve is now leaning towards easing, hardly a recipe for a big rally. \par \par \uc2  While Japan's still huge trade surplus with the United States makes a major turnaround in the dollar/yen unlikely, it still has more scope to rally than the dollar/mark. \par \par \uc2  "In the case of the yen there are more grounds for optimism," DKB's Stamenkovic said. "But we have been seeing Japanese portfolio flows diversifying into German assets and if that continues it is going to give some support to the mark." \par \par \uc2  "Also the mark is not as overbought against the dollar as the yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr7c05zuc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
