\par\par \b \uc2  G7 will continue to seek strong dlr - MOF official. \b0\par\par\uc2 389 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 1 (Reuter) - The Group of Seven (G7) major nations will continue to seek a strong dollar from now on, but whether they will conduct more joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon is not certain, a senior official at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "was in line with the content of the G7 communique (released on April 25). The policy among major nations of desiring a strong dollar may continue for a while," the official said. "But I cannot say whether they will conduct yet more joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon as they need to seek good timing (for such an action)," he added. \par \par \uc2  The G7 leaders would reaffirm \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation but might not discuss topics such as setting up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target zones when they meet in Canada in mid-June, the official said. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 summit meeting may discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability but something like target zones would not be a topic. The meeting would merely reaffirm \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coooperation," he said. \par \par \uc2  The ministry official said the United States, Japan and European nations were able to take joint action in supporting the dollar on Wednesday, as each nation's interest was in the same direction. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied overnight after 12 central banks bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that caught markets by surprise. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in keeping with "the exchange rate objectives in the April 25 communique of the G7". Takemura's ministry repeated Rubin's comment in a statement late on Wednesday evening. \par \par \uc2  The ministry official said the United States, already facing signs of slowdown in its economy, was concerned about stagflation in case a weak dollar triggered inflation from more expensive imports, the MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  European leaders, especially newly elected French President Jacques Chirac, have been trying to establish a single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and want to avoid any instability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the ministry official said. \par \par \uc2  Japan, meanwile, had its own problems with the strong yen, as fears about a stagnant domestic economy were growing in stock and credit markets at home, he added. \par \par \uc2  "Economic circumstances in Japan, the United States and Europe were in the same direction, so they were able to take the concerted action overnight," the official said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr6107cbm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
