\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar hits new post-war low, then recovers. \b0\par\par\uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuter) - The dollar touched a new post-World War Two low of 79.75 yen Wednesday morning but recovered after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  Dealers blamed speculative selling as well as pessimism over U.S.-Japan talks on auto trade for the latest decline, which pushed the dollar below its previous record low of 80.15 yen, set less than 10 days ago in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought the dollar off its lows to just above 80.00 yen, and in early New York trading it had moved up to 81.35 yen. \par \par \uc2  "There isn't a sense yet that the dollar's fall has been overdone, which may push it down further to the lower end of the 79-yen band in the near term," said a bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now plummeted more than 20 percent this year, from a high of 101.45 yen in January. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Tomiichi Murayama said the yen's steep rise was troublesome but he believed it was only a temporary phenomenon, and pledged that Japan would decisively implement promised economic stimulus steps such as an extra budget for this fiscal year, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  A senior Bank of Japan official told Reuters that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take "strong action" to combat the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets have judged as too late and too vague a Japanese emergency economic package, approved on Friday, and a simultaneous cut in the BOJ's discount rate by 0.75 percentage point to a new low of one percent. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts are sceptical about whether Tokyo has any strong options left to halt the rampaging yen, which threatens to scuttle Japan's economic recovery and send its battered stock market into fresh declines. \par \par \uc2  Selling pressure on the dollar was also rekindled after U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura failed over the weekend to come up with any concrete steps to boost the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The two met in Indonesia on the fringes of a broader meeting of Asian-Pacific finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  Many economists doubt that the U.S. Federal Reserve will raise interest rates before this summer to defend the dollar, or that Washington will take bold steps to cut America's huge budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  Dealers, who suspect the United States may be tolerating a weak dollar to pry trade concessions out of Japan, said lack of progress in U.S.-Japan auto trade talks in Washington weighed down the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. Undersecretary of Commerce Jeffrey Garten said on Tuesday after two days of talks that "wide gaps" remained between Japanese and U.S. positions on car trade. \par \par \uc2  Garten and other negotiators shied away from talking about possible sanctions, but U.S. Trade Representative Mickey Kantor has threatened to move ahead with trade sanctions if talks fail to produce a deal to open Japan's car parts market to more foreign imports. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials said after the latest round of talks that they might walk out and take the dispute to the new World Trade Organisation in Geneva if Washington moves ahead with sanctions. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4j08m6z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
