\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Takemura -3: Monetary Policy,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Effective \b0\par\par\uc2 172 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 1995\par 08:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Finance minister Takemura said the meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the Group of Seven (G-7) industrial nations scheduled for later in April will debate what policy steps are needed to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Takemura said monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market international will remain important tools for ensuring stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. But those tools alone won't be enough, he said, reiterating that a debate on a fundamental review of the floating exchange rate system may be needed. \par \par \uc2  Takemura said Japan will work hard to come up with details of the economic stimulus package it outlined last Friday, including presenting a supplementary budget over the next month. \par \par \uc2  Asked about reports that Vice Finance Minister Jiro Saito could resign in the near future, Takemura said nothing has been decided. But looking at the parliamentary schedule, a personnel shift at the ministry could take place in late May, he said. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 19-04-95 \par \par \uc2  0634GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr4j02rzc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
