\par\par \b \uc2  Australian, Malaysian, NZ markets defy doldrums. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, April 18 (Reuter) - The Australian, New Zealand and Malaysian markets defied the post-holiday doldrums gripping most Asian bourses on Tuesday, as the dollar fell through the critical 81.50-yen support level. \par \par \uc2  Trade was subdued in Tokyo and Hong Kong, with the Nikkei ending down 0.48 percent and the Hang Seng off 0.20 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Australian market surged to a five-month high, boosted by afternoon buying of major banks and resource issues. The All Ordinaries Index closed up 19.8 points at 2040.5, its highest level since November 1, 1994. \par \par \uc2  New Zealand shares also climbed on record turnover, propelled by the continued bid by International Paper for control of forestry and paper group Carter Holt Harvey. The NZSE-40 Capital Index ended up 21.21 points, or 1.01 percent, at 2,114.32. Turnover was an all-time high of more than NZ$1.118 billion, an NZSE spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  A technical bounce pushed up the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange's Composite Index 1.67 points to 964.30 in a choppy session, brokers said, with bargain hunting alternating with bouts of forced selling. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the Nikkei 225-share average closed down 79.04 points to 16,225.11. Volume was remarkably low at some 150 million shares. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market lacks direction as investors are keeping away due to the yen's strength and fears that the dollar might plunge further. \par \par \uc2  "Everything very much hangs upon the yen-dollar level. And it looks like we're once again going to test the 80 yen to the dollar level," said Tim Shanagher, head of sales at Schroders. \par \par \uc2  Concern over the U.S. dollar's weakness also kept buyers at bay in Hong Kong, dealers said. The blue-chip Hang Seng Index dipped 17.02 points to 8,644.97. \par \par \uc2  "There wasn't really very much selling. It's just that buying interest was weak on concern over the U.S. dollar," said Samuel Lau of Seapower Research. "Sentiment was weak also because it was the first day of trade after the holidays." \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan, Singapore and Seoul markets, as well as many smaller bourses, eased on lack of interest or profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  The dollar continued to sag in late Tokyo trade after breaking through the key 81.50-yen support level earlier in the day. Despite several Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the greenback was at 81.36 yen at 1030 GMT, down from the local open of 81.80/90 yen and the New York close of 81.83/93 yen. \par \par \uc2  "I'd say the dollar could well try the downside and set a new low within the next week or two," one European bank dealer said. "But its weakening will be gradual, slowed by the BOJ and probably light bids, as we saw today." \par \par \uc2  Gold closed at the day's high in Hong Kong on late overseas buying, dealers said. Bullion ended at US$393.80/394.30 an ounce in quiet trade, against New York's $393.50/394.00 close and the local opening of $393.20/70. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil prices inched higher in Singapore, with June Brent bid at about $18.15 and offered at about $18.20, up five cents from the New York close of $18.10/$18.15. \par \par \uc2  Following are the market highlights in Asia: \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG - Hong Kong shares ended slightly lower in very quiet trade with buying interest absent amid concern over the U.S. dollar's weakness, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index dipped 17.02 points, or 0.20 percent, to 8,644.97. \par \par \uc2  "There wasn't really very much selling. It's just that buying interest was weak on concern over the U.S. dollar," said Samuel Lau of Seapower Research. "Sentiment was weak also because it was the first day of trade after the holidays." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japanese stocks ended soft after a subdued day of narrow trading. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average closed down 79.04 points, or 0.48 percent, to 16,225.11. Volume was remarkably low at some 150 million shares. \par \par \uc2  "Everything very much hangs upon the yen-dollar level," said Tim Shanagher, head of sales at Schroders. "And it looks like we're once again going to test the 80 yen to the dollar level." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK - Thai stocks closed lower after investors dumped finance and securities issues over concerns their earnings may be diluted after the implementation of large capital increases in new share issues, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The SET index slid 16.30 points to end at 1,177.83 but off the day's low of 1,172.91 in thin trade. A meagre 37 million shares worth 2.84 billion baht changed hands. \par \par \uc2  "Investors sold finance issues in the afternoon when they were panicked by a lot of new rights issue shares from firms that are soon entering the market," an Ekachart Finance broker said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY - Shares on the Bombay bourse ended lower, with brokers saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credit policy announced on Monday would prompt banks to raise their interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share Bombay Stock Exchange index ended at a provisional 3,427.87, shedding 32.59 points from its previous close. The composite National index fell 19.84 points to a provisional close of 1,663.53. \par \par \uc2  "Volumes are poor since everyone has adopted a cautious approach. We are expecting banks to raise their interest rates," said Antony Sequeira of brokers Uday S. Kotak. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  COLOMBO - Sri Lanka stocks drifted lower and closed down in the absence of market-moving news and tight liquidity, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Colombo Stock Exchange all share index fell 2.22 points to 810.09. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR - Share prices closed higher after a choppy session as bargain hunting alternated with bouts of forced selling, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The key Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange's Composite Index ended up 1.67 points at 964.30. \par \par \uc2  "I think most of the forced selling has been cleared and we may see further improvement tomorrow," said a dealer at a local brokerage. "Buying was quite strong today." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MANILA - Philippine shares drifted lower as domestic investors lightened their blue-chip holdings to take short-term profits from the pre-Easter rally, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share composite index closed 10.32 points weaker at 2,477.630. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - Shares closed easier after profit-taking wiped out early gains in thin trading, brokers said. The composite stock index shed 1.83 points to 917.06, off a day high of 924.93. \par \par \uc2  "The market fell on a technical adjustment. But many blue chips remained firm. Samsung Electronics and Hyundai Motor came into the spotlight," said Choi Hyeon-chol of First Securities. \par \par \uc2  But H.K. Kang of Schroders said: "The worst is not over. The market needs to retreat to around the 850 level. Once it hits the level, the market will turn around by itself." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI - Shanghai's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B shares closed little changed in sluggish trading with most investors sidelined on the first day after the Easter holiday, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The B share index edged down 0.076 points, or 0.14 percent, to 54.390 on thin volume of 4.476 million shares. But volume was up from Monday's 1.164 million shares, which was the lowest this year. \par \par \uc2  "Many investors were still in holiday mood," said one broker. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's domestic A share index dipped 6.144 points, or 0.94 percent, to 647.669 points on thin turnover of 418.942 million yuan. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHENZHEN - Shenzhen A and B shares ended lower in response to an announcement that foreign holding companies will not be permitted to invest in A shares and after a weak performance by Pudong-related shares in Shanghai, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The A share index slipped 1.08 points to 136.25 on turnover of 155.79 million yuan while the B share index slipped 0.26 to 73.64 on a turnover of HK$1.51 million. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Singapore share prices closed mostly flat, with brokers lamenting a general disinterest in local stocks by foreign institutional funds. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share Straits Times Industrials index ended down 2.64 points at 2,051.09. \par \par \uc2  "I looked at the market index and it hardly moved the whole day. My clients were just the same -- they refused to budge," a foreign firm's institutional dealer said. He said he expects the market to fall five percent from current levels. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - The Australian market surged to finish at a five-month high, boosted by afternoon buying of major banks and resource issues. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries Index closed up 19.8 points at 2040.5, its highest level since November 1, 1994. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said an absence of sellers and the return of offshore players to the market helped boost trade to a flurry after the midsession. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI - Taiwan share prices rebounded slightly from recent heavy losses to close mixed, but market sentiment remained bearish over weak financials, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  After falling to an intra-day low of 6,050.81, the weighted index recovered to close down 5.51 points, or 0.09 percent, at 6,106.56. \par \par \uc2  The market opened higher on bargain hunting after the index fell by more than 100 points on both Monday and Saturday. The boost was short-lived as financials continued to plunge on weak fundamentals and slow profit expectations, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON - The continued bid by International Paper for control of forestry and paper group Carter Holt Harvey propelled New Zealand shares to their highest point since late last year on record turnover. \par \par \uc2  The NZSE-40 Capital Index ended up 21.21 points, or 1.01 percent, at 2,114.32. The bid boosted turnover to more than NZ$1.118 billion, its highest ever, an NZSE spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  By the close, more than 271.99 million CHH shares, worth NZ$1.033 billion. IP, which is offering NZ$3.80 a share, needs 323 million shares to achieve its objective of 50.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4i08k6n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
