\par\par \b \uc2  Japan urges  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  system review as dlr plunges. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 10 (Reuter) - Japan's finance minister called on Monday for a review of the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system as the U.S. dollar plunged to record lows against the yen, threatening the Japanese economy's shaky recovery. \par \par \uc2  Panic selling pushed the dollar, which began the year at 101.45 yen, to a post-World War Two low of 80.15 yen in Tokyo morning trade. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had closed at 83.68/75 in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) later pushed the dollar back up to near 83 yen, but government officials said the chaos in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets threatened to inflict severe damage on Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura, in apparent frustration with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market gyrations, told a parliamentary panel that the system under which exchange rates float freely, adopted in 1973, should be re-thought. \par \par \uc2  "In order to counter the current rapid appreciation of the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to emphasise policy coordination and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other nations," he said, urging the United States to do more to defend the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "But we also need to think whether we can leave the current exchange rate system as it is now," he said. \par \par \uc2  Takemura noted some economists in other nations had begun discussing the possibility of reforms to the system, although analysts in Tokyo doubted major reforms would be practical. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think this is a serious proposal. Attempts to fix exchange rates (in Europe) in the last couple of years have been extremely unsuccessful and to try this now, with so much uncertainty at the global level, strikes me as impossible," said Robert Feldman, chief economist at Salomon Brothers Asia. \par \par \uc2  Top government spokesman Kozo Igarashi said the yen's rise cast a shadow over the economy. "Since the economy is just showing signs of recovery, the yen's rise should intensify worries about the situation," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Igarashi said Prime Minister Tomiichi Murayama would ask the government's Economic Planning Agency to draw up emergency economic steps this week to cope with the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The sharp appreciation of the yen tends to squeeze profits at Japan's exporters and hurt their competitiveness abroad. \par \par \uc2  Igarashi said the plan would be "comprehensive and detailed", but did not specify what steps it would contain. \par \par \uc2  Economists believe it might include more government spending to stimulate the economy, financial aid for smaller companies, looser monetary policy and a cut in the stock transaction tax to revive the weak share market. \par \par \uc2  Some economists said, however, that the government was neither able nor willing to come up with measures that would address one key, underlying cause of the yen's surge -- Japan's intransigent trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  "My impression is the government doesn't understand the problem, and the solutions they are proposing are not strong enough," said Jeff Young, analyst at Salomon Brothers Asia. \par \par \uc2  "On the fiscal side, don't expect a whole lot and on the monetary side, the BOJ has just eased...and if it eases again, is it really going to make that much difference? Probably not." \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 31 said it would ease money market rates, disappointing financial markets which had hoped for the more drastic move of an early discount rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Speculation is mounting that the BOJ will cut its discount rate, the rate it charges on loans to commercial banks, by 0.75 percentage point from its current record low of 1.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4a08u51\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
