\par\par \b \uc2  Japan wants review of global  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  system - MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 10 (Reuter) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) wants to reconsider the global system of floating exchange rates, Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura told the financial settlements committee of the upper house of parliament. \par \par \uc2  "In order to counter the current rapid appreciation of the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to emphasise policy coordination and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other nations," Takemura said. \par \par \uc2  "But we also need to think whether we can leave the current exchange rate system as it is now," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The international community has already started discussions on what is the problem behind the current rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, which are not in line with economic fundamentals, as well as on the present floating exchange rate system." \par \par \uc2  Takemura said, "We would like to pay close attention to that topic now." \par \par \uc2  He also said Japan must try to make the yen more of an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the medium to long term. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4a08u3c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
