\par\par \b \uc2  Leading central banks try to boost dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Leonard Santorelli \par \uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 5 (Reuter) - The world's financial giants -- the United States, Germany and Japan -- stepped in to prop up the dollar on Wednesday but first signs were that the move had only limited success. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which slid earlier in the day to near record lows against the yen, found little comfort in the concerted action by the U.S. Federal Reserve, German Bundesbank and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , barely managing to hang on to its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  The move by the three heavyweights buoyed the dollar temporarily and it rallied above 1.38 marks and 86 yen but the rally petered out on lack of follow-through buying, dealers in London said. \par \par \uc2  "It clearly looks as if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not worked," said David Cocker, treasury adviser at Chemical Bank. "It proves it is very difficult to change sentiment through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Most other European central banks shied away from joining the fray. Dealers said the Bank of France was also seen buying dollars for marks but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  The buying marked the first round of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. So far this year, the dollar has fallen more than 13 percent against the Japanese yen and 11 percent against the German mark. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the Fed bought about $1 billion for marks and yen. But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Frankfurt said the Bundesbank probably  only half-heartedly joined in the round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The head of trading at one major Frankfurt bank said he did not believe the dollar-buying by the Bundesbank was particularly heavy, in contrast to what U.S. dealers described as aggressive buying by the U.S. Fed. \par \par \uc2  In London, the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Kleinwort Bensen, Alan Mackenzie, told Reuters Television he suspected that the intevention came at the request of Washington. \par \par \uc2  Manfred Kunert, head of trading at Frankfurt's DG Bank, agreed. "I don't think it was the Bundesbank's own idea. They were asked to join in. On its own the Bundesbank would not have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Frankfurt said the dollar's only tepid rise on the back of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also suggested that the volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least by the Bundesbank, was limited. \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank has regularly voiced scepticism about the value of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but does say it will not shy away from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market action if this is requested. \par \par \uc2  "It's a week too late," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy in London. "They would have been much better off doing it last week to coordinate with the Bundesbank cut." \par \par \uc2  The dollar had earlier been knocked hard by comments from German Finance Minister Theo Waigel that cooperation among Group of Seven industrial nations on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was no substitute for correct national economic policies. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Europe had a dull day. London stocks, which closed at their best level this year on the previous day, edged up only a little. In Frankfurt, stocks rose slightly unable to shake off worries about the weak dollar and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  STOCKS \par \par \uc2  The Financial Times-Stock Exchange index of 100 leading British shares closed up 2.1 points at 3,190.2. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 share index dropped 9.85 to 1,872.92. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share DAX index in Frankfurt rose 4.83 at 1,969.84 and then rose in post-bourse tradse to 1.972.59. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1.3821 marks and 86.33 yen after closing in Europe on Tuesday at 1.3859 and 86.40. \par \par \uc2  GOLD \par \par \uc2  Gold closed at $392.00 an ounce, up 50 cents from Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4508xu5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
