\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Short-Covering Eases Pressure On Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By MARI PFEIFFER \par \uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 1995\par 07:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (AP-DJ)--Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and persistent short-covering in Asian dealings Tuesday eased some of selling pressure that knocked the dollar to new postwar lows against the yen. \par \par \uc2  But expectations for the dollar to fall further remain 'perfectly intact,' said a dealer at a Japanese long-term credit bank in Tokyo, noting that Japanese exporters have yet to come out in full force in the remote hopes of a slightly stronger U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively from early in the Asian day below 86.00 yen, and were probably buying dollars throughout the day to prevent a fall below 86.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a new postwar low of 85.60 yen early Tuesday in Australasian dealings. \par \par \uc2  Around 0533 GMT, the dollar is quoted at 86.24 yen, above 86.13 yen late Monday in New York and 86.18 yen late Monday in London. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also is at 1.3722 Deutsche marks, lower than 1.3724 marks late Monday in New York and 1.3726 marks in London Monday. \par \par \uc2  The mark is quoted at 62.85 yen, up from 62.76 yen in New York late Monday and 62.79 yen in London late Monday. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar's misery has been most pronounced against its major counterparts, the strength of some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided the dollar with a one-two punch, requiring \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hit a new record high amid continued dollar sales, while the Bank of Indonesia sold dollars in both the spot and the forward markets. Some traders said in addition to general pessimism toward the dollar, some Asian countries that have borrowed funds from Japan for infrastructure-related projects are concerned about increasing yen their official reserves. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Tuesday, Japan's Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura said he thinks the yen should become a more internationalized \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in Asia, alluding pessimism surrounding the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the high rate of borrowing by Asian countries. \par \par \uc2  The unusual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday in Asia that coupled the U.S. Federal Reserve and the Bank of Japan in a mission to save the dollar has been viewed largely as ineffective, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday was perceived differently, some said. 'The market is really quite short, and people are talking about U.S. fund players covering their short positions,' said a dealer at a European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  He added that amid speculation the Bank of Japan may cut its official discount rate as soon as this week, many fund managers are displaying a fair amount of caution. \par \par \uc2  'I'm looking for the dollar to trade between 85.50 yen and 86.50 yen for the next 24 hours,' the European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He and other dealers said 85.50 yen will remain the dollar's most crucial support level in the near term. 'For some U.S. funds, 85.50 yen is a knock out option point that will undoubtedly trigger some heavy stop-loss selling. After that, I guess the market will target 80.00 yen,' the long-term credit bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The market remains convinced Japan's huge trade surpluses will guarantee a weaker dollar, and hopes for coordinated monetary policy among the Group of Seven (G-7) nations are dwindling, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 04-04-95 \par \par \uc2  0541GMT \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr4402dd1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
