\par\par \b \uc2  (WSJ): Rate Boost Seen As Only Step Left For U.S. Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2  By MICHAEL R. SESIT \par \uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 1995\par 05:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (AP-DJ)--Only higher U.S. interest rates can revive the ailing dollar now. That, at least, is the growing belief of traders and analysts who watched the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sink to yet another post-World War II low of 85.85 yen, The Wall Street Journal reports Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The slide occurred despite about $2 billion in estimated dollar purchases by the U.S. Federal Reserve (Fed) and Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Avinash Persaud, an international economist at J.P. Morgan & Co. in London notes that the dollar remains sickly, despite the Bundesbank's surprise cut in German interest rates Thursday, recent upward revisions in U.S. fourth-quarter growth and more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Fed. \par \par \uc2  In addition to yesterday's purchases to support the dollar, analysts say Bank of Japan data show that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent as much as a record $14 billion last month trying to halt the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  And in a rare admission by U.S. authorities, Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin yesterday confirmed that the Fed had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian trading - itself an uncommon occurrence. (The New York Fed, which conducts such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says the last time it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian trading was in February 1992.) \par \par \uc2  Rubin also reaffirmed the administration's commitment to a stronger dollar. \par \par \uc2  Persaud believes that - despite an 'illusion' on the part of some investors that the U.S. economy will experience a 'soft' landing - inflation pressures could prompt the Fed to raise rates at its Federal Open Market Committee meeting on May 23. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 03-04-95 \par \par \uc2  0334GMT \par \par \uc2  1125 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr4402d5b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
