\par\par \b \uc2  =US  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2 /Dlr -2-: BOJ Official Welcomes Action \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 1995\par 02:00\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  But the Fed, focusing on signs of a slowing economy at home, didn't lift U.S. interest rates at a meeting of policymakers last week and isn't expected to do so anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  Treasury and Fed officials, and private economists as well, say economic fundamentals don't justify the dollar's remarkable and persistent decline. "It's getting ridiculous - the strength of the yen," said economist Jeffrey Frankel of the Institute for International Economics, a Washington think tank. He added, "We may be approaching the mirror image of 1985," when the dollar soared, causing U.S. exporters the same sort of pain that their Japanese counterparts are now feeling. \par \par \uc2  At some point, Frankel said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will decide that the mark and yen have risen too far. And when that moment comes, coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strong public statements by a number of governments can be an important influence on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, just as they were in the 1985 accord which helped bring down the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But so far, finance ministers and central bankers from Germany, Japan and the U.S. haven't engaged in any high-profile public expressions of their shared conviction that the dollar is too low and the mark and yen too high. Finance ministers and central bankers from the three countries are scheduled to meet with their counterparts from Britain, Canada, France and Italy in Washington on April 25. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock and bond markets shrugged off the renewed decline of the dollar. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 10.72 to 4168.41. The yield on 30-year Treasury bonds slipped to 7.40% from 7.43% Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, where the surging yen is threatening a fragile economic recovery and frightening the stock market, a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  official welcomed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "timely and appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Rubin, in his statement, also said the Clinton administration remains "committed to strengthening the fundamentals that are ultimately important for maintaining a strong and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," including "continued progress" in reducing the federal deficit and increasing national savings. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 04-03-95 \par \par \uc2  2000EDT \par \par \uc2  4205 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011028dr44000uc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
