\par\par \b \uc2  JGBs end up over one point in Tokyo as stocks sink. \b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuter) - Key June Japanese government bond (JGB) futures jumped over one point in Tokyo from Friday's close on active short-covering, reflecting the fragile stock market and the continued strength of the yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The June ended at 115.75, the highest close in Tokyo since January 24, 1994, when the key contract ended at 115.95. \par \par \uc2  "There's nothing to stop this bullish trend," said a commercial bank trader. "The market is now very hesitant about holding short positions because prices keep on rising." \par \par \uc2  The June moved in a range of 115.03-115.92, with volume of 58,062 contracts. It closed at 114.56 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Stocks' plunge and the yen's strength cheered sentiment," said another bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Active buying and aggressive short-covering by investors emerged in early morning trade in response to the sharp falls in the Tokyo stock market. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average closed down 758.66 points at 15,381.29, its lowest finish since 15,267.76 on August 20, 1992. \par \par \uc2  The yen's bullishness against the dollar also encouraged buying in the bond market. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained strong despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) and the U.S. Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  "The bond market is expected to stay strong as the effect of the yen's rise and weak stocks will likely have a negative effect on the economy," a trust bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  Market operators were willing to pick up bonds on dips, given this bullishness. Many traders are starting to factor in a 75 basis point cut in the Japanese discount rate. \par \par \uc2  "Investors were seen buying long bonds aggressively, reflecting the falls in short-term rates," said the second bank trader. "(The June) still has the potential to rise as the market is starting to factor in a 75 basis point cut in the discount rate. A 50 bps cut is not enough anymore." \par \par \uc2  The yield of the benchmark 174th 10-year bond dropped nearly 20 basis points to 3.395 percent at 0749 GMT. Its Friday close was 3.580. \par \par \uc2  Cash bonds kept their strength throughout the day in line with the bullishness in the futures market. Active short-covering buying by city banks was seen. \par \par \uc2  Japan plans to hold an auction of six-year government bonds on Tuesday. A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official said the MOF is considering offering about the same volume as in the last auction. The MOF last offered about 500 billion yen of the bonds in February. -- Chikafumi Hodo +81-3-3432-8785 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4309414\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
