\par\par \b \uc2  G3 inaction leaves dollar praying for a miracle. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Young \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 3 (Reuter) - A growing view that the U.S. and Japan have missed out on a golden opportunity to rescue the dollar has got many analysts wondering just what can be done to save the ailing U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from further torment. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's failure to cut the ODR rate following the Bundesbank's unexpected reduction last week in German interest rates provided yet another excuse to dump the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If the authorities (Group-of-Three) were serious about turning the dollar then they have missed the opportunity," said Paul Lambert, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at UBS in London. \par \par \uc2  "They could have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and changed monetary policy on a coordinated basis. But the market thinks there is still a U.S. policy of benign neglect towards the dollar," said Lambert. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's announcement last week that although it would nudge short term interest rates lower this was not a prelude to a cut in the discount rate, sent the dollar skidding to yet another post-world war two record low against the yen of 86.05, around 13 percent down from the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve also disappointed dollar bulls by leaving U.S. monetary policy on hold at last week's Federal Open Market Committee meeting, although this decision was expected. \par \par \uc2  "With the Fed passing up on its opportunity to tighten last week, and with the BOJ sending ambiguous signals on interest rates, the room for manoeuvre now looks extremely limited," said Tim Fox, an economist at Credit Suisse in London. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts are sceptical a U.S. rate rise would bail out the dollar anyway given big trade and budget deficits and the U.S.'s dependence on foreign capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, there are serious doubts the Fed would raise rates just to support the dollar when the U.S. equity and bond markets have performed so well of late. \par \par \uc2  However, most analysts are at least agreed that further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar will provide nothing more than a short-term prop. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve dollar purchases on Monday in Asia, the first by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since February 1992, alongside the more customary buying from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to make much of an impression. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't have any significant long-term effect," said Shahaab Ialinoos, economist at IBJ International here. \par \par \uc2  "People are now worried that we are now seeing a long-term downtrend that will be difficult to reverse even through short-term interest rate moves," he added. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. action prompted speculation that European central banks might enter the fray in Europe. The last time this occurred was on March 3 when 15 European central banks, led by the Bundesbank, joined the Fed and the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  This failed to halt the dollar's slide and given the BOJ's massive dollar buying spree in March to the tune of 14 billion dollars any action from the European central banks is likely to prove wholly ineffective, said analysts. \par \par \uc2  From which ever angle investors look at the dollar the near to medium term outlook remains bleak. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporter and institutional dollar sales have showed little sign of slowing despite the start of the financial year. Japanese institutions normally repatriate funds for balance sheet reasons ahead of the March 31 financial year-end. \par \par \uc2  There is also a clear lack of progress in U.S./Japan trade talks and Mexico's dire financial situation continues to rumble on in the background. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will test the lows on the mark and forge new lows on yen unless there is some catalyst to turn sentiment, and that is conspicious by its absence at present," said Lambert. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a record low of 1.3450 marks on March 8, some 11 percent down from the start of 1995. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr43093t0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
