\par\par \b \uc2  Asian markets mixed on profit-taking, thin volume. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, March 31 (Reuter) - Asian markets were mixed on Friday, with trading characterised by profit-taking and thin volume, as the U.S. dollar moved erratically beneath the New York close. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo, Hong Kong and Singapore were the major losers, with the Nikkei falling 2.25 percent amid disappointment at the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) announcement that it would guide money market rates lower but leave the discount rate unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Japan's overnight interest rate between banks plunged from 2.25 percent to a record low of 1.75 percent, equalling the discount rate. \par \par \uc2  "Expectations for a rate cut were mounting up until early this morning," said Masayuki Matsumoto, market analyst at Dai-Ichi Securities. "Some speculated an official discount rate cut may come as early as next week, but the BOJ's policy stance disappointed investors." \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average closed a volatile day down 372.27 points at 16,139.95, after rising as high as 16,848.13 in the morning before the BOJ's announcement. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, the blue-chip Hang Seng Index fell one percent, tumbling below the 8,600 level on profit-taking amid what brokers called a lack of fresh positive factors. The Hang Seng Index slipped 88.45 points to close at 8,587.72. \par \par \uc2  "Trade has largely lost direction after yesterday's index futures settlement and much of the good news has been discounted," said Antony Mak, an associate sales director at Vickers Ballas. "It looks like the market will head lower in the short term for want of new reasons to buy." \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the Hong Kong bourse's rally in recent weeks has fully reflected the property market's revival and Wall Street's record run, which has begun to strike some players as unsustainable. \par \par \uc2  Singapore shares also finished lower, with thin losses on lack of institutional buying support, brokers said. The 30-share Straits Times Industrials index fell 8.71 points, or 0.41 percent, to 2,093.12. \par \par \uc2  Among the winners, Australian shares closed at their highest level since February 28, boosted by the release of weaker-than-expected December quarter gross domestic product (GDP) data which took pressure off interest rates, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries index ended up 22 points, or 1.17 percent, at 1906.6, just off its intra-day high of 1908.2. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell against the yen and the mark by late Tokyo trade, despite the BOJ's moves to guide short-term rates lower and to shore up the dollar through desperate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tokyo dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Outpacing selling pressure by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exporters and others was seen crippling the BOJ's actions on interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a European bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was being bid at 87.15 yen at 0645 GMT after opening in Tokyo at 89.42 against New York's closing 89.58. \par \par \uc2  Gold closed unchanged in Hong Kong after a sharp overnight gain in New York, dealers said. Bullion ended at US$385.80/386.30 an ounce against New York's $385.90/386.40 close. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil prices drifted quietly, with May Brent bid at $17.36 in late Singapore trade. \par \par \uc2  These are the Asian market highlights: \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japanese stocks and Nikkei futures closed sharply lower amid disappointment at the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) announcement it would guide money market rates lower but leave the discount rate unchanged, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average closed a volatile day down 372.27 points, or 2.25 percent, at 16,139.95, after rising as high as 16,848.13 before the BOJ announcement. \par \par \uc2  After Germany's rate cut on Thursday, investors had hoped for a discount rate cut in Japan and sentiment was damaged severely when one failed to materialise, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG - Hong Kong stocks tumbled below the 8,600 level at the close on profit-taking amid a lack of fresh positive factors, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index slipped 88.45 points, or one percent, to close at 8,587.72 on turnover of HK$3.07 billion. \par \par \uc2  "Trade has largely lost direction after yesterday's index futures settlement and much of the good news has been discounted," said Antony Mak, an associate sales director at Vickers Ballas. "It looks like the market will head lower in the short term for want of new reasons to buy." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Singapore share prices finished with thin losses on lack of institutional buying support, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share Straits Times Industrials index fell 8.71 points, or 0.41 percent, to 2,093.12. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think we will see buying orders return, not so soon," a chief dealer with a local brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK - Thai stocks closed lower on scattered profit-taking as a gloomy mood prevailed despite an early rise on the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's discount rate cut, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The SET index closed down 4.04 points at 1,216.68 after fluctuating between 1,211.90 and 1,227.68. \par \par \uc2  "Investors just didn't believe the index could go any higher and decided to take short-term profits," a Bara Development broker said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY - Lack of support from the usual big buyers combined with sporadic selling pressure from the country's biggest mutual fund, Unit Trust of India, to pull down Bombay share prices, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share Bombay Stock Exchange index fell 27.36 points, or 0.83 percent, to 3,260.96. The 100-share index slipped 3.94 points to 1,605.17. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  COLOMBO - Sri Lanka stocks closed marginally up after 10 days of declines, with investors supporting prices to dress up balance sheets for the March 31 year-end, operators said. \par \par \uc2  The Colombo Stock Exchange all share index rose 1.73 points to 834.88. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA - The Jakarta stock market was closed due to the fiscal year-end and will resume trading on Monday. The official index ended Thursday 0.28 points lower at 428.64. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MANILA - The Philippine market survived an early threat of technical correction as selected issues rallied on persistent buying from foreign institutional investors, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The composite index climbed 19.90 points, or 0.84 percent, to close at 2,392.25. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the last-minute rise was triggered by continued foreign buying of phone stock PLDT. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - A late spurt of institutional buying pushed Seoul stocks to a higher close in thin and range-bound trading, brokers said. The composite stock index added 4.85 points to 931.78. \par \par \uc2  "Institutional investors, due to close their books today, placed buy orders across the board toward the close," said Park Key-pyong of Hanyang Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Today's rise was nothing but a technical rebound following a four-day-long decline," Choi Hyeon-cheol of First Securities said. "The market prospects still look negative." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI - Shanghai's B shares, continuing Thursday's consolidation, ended slightly higher in slow trade with investors sidelined by lack of fresh news, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The B index gained 0.010 points, or 0.02 percent, to 57.168 on tiny volume of 4.566 million shares. \par \par \uc2  "Major institutions were sidelined, leaving the market to trading by small investors who play for small margins," one broker said. \par \par \uc2  The domestic A share index was led by Pudong developers and ended higher at 669.359 points, up 17.230 points or 2.64 percent, on turnover of 1.190 billion yuan. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHENZHEN - Shenzhen A shares closed marginally higher. B shares ended little changed. \par \par \uc2  The A share index rose 2.00 points, or 1.46 percent, to 139.12 while turnover slackened to 247.09 million yuan. The B share index rose 0.01 point to 74.12. The composite index rose 1.85 points to 135.29. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the slight rise in the A shares was linked to the Shenzhen Stock Exchange's announcement that it will boost fees for trading in risky bond futures. Technical buying also added to the rise, they said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - Australian shares closed near their highs on turnover boosted by option expiries, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries index ended up 22 points, or 1.17 percent, at 1906.6, just off its intra-day high of 1908.2. It was the biggest rise since February 28. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market was boosted by the release of weaker-than-expected December quarter gross domestic product data which took pressure off interest rates. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI - Taiwan share prices closed mixed in active trade as profit-taking emerged in electronics and plastics after their previous gains, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The index ended up 9.54 points, or 0.15 percent, at 6,524.00. \par \par \uc2  The market opened higher on news that the Securities and Exchange Commission approved the creation of three new securities investment trust firms. But market belief that electronics and plastics had reached their short-term highs led to heavy selling, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON - New Zealand share prices ended the March financial year on a firm note in mostly leader-driven trade. \par \par \uc2  The NZSE-40 Capital Index ended 6.69 points higher at 1,997.53 on turnover worth NZ$51.4 million. \par \par \uc2  Matthew Rose at Hendry Hay McIntosh said that with bonds performing better, the equities market should consolidate with a firm bias next week. \par \par \uc2  "We are mildly optimistic on a short-term basis," Rose said. "The market has got good underlying strength." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr3v091mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
