\par\par \b \uc2  Japan stance of proper  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  steps unchanged - MOF. \b0\par\par\uc2 236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 20 (Reuter) - Japan's stance of taking timely and appropriate steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains the same despite the dollar's fall to a new record low, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Asked if Japan will continue to take timely and appropriate steps on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while keeping in touch with other Group of Seven (G7) nations, the senior MOF official said: "Yes, of course. Our stance remains the same." \par \par \uc2  The yen's latest rise to a new record of 88.65 yen to the dollar in early Asian trading has been "too rapid" and this does not reflect economic fundamentals, the official said. \par \par \uc2  "The move was speculative," the official said. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans any special action besides dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, he said, "I'm not in a position to answer that question." \par \par \uc2  "You need to keep a close watch on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for a while, since the yen retreated slightly from its early highs later this morning," the MOF official said, when asked about his view of the strong yen and slumping Tokyo stock prices. \par \par \uc2  Early this morning, the Nikkei share average briefly slipped below 16,000 for the first time since November 1993 because of bearish sentiment worsened by the dollar's renewed decline. It later recouped some losses. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par 