\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Takemura -4:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Has Had Impact Many Times \b0\par\par\uc2 247 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par 02:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Takemura said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to continue efforts to calm the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as it keeps in close contact with its U.S. and European partners. He also noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market has proven effective many times in the past in countering overly speculative or volatile activity. \par \par \uc2  Takemura said he spoke to U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin several times by telephone on March 3, and he also met with France's Economics Minister Edmond Alphandery, who happened to be in Tokyo that day. Alphandery, meanwhile, spoke by telephone that night with German monetary authorities. Together, the finance chiefs reached a consensus that coordinated action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was necessary, he said. \par \par \uc2  But the Group of Seven (G-7) finance ministers and central bankers didn't decide to convene an emergency meeting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil at that time. Takemura earlier this month had said he wanted to consider the possibility of holding such a meeting, but he later clarified his remarks to say that he didn't intend to imply that he was making a call for the gathering. Tuesday in parliament Takemura said he can't say with 100% certainty that the G-7 aren't thinking about such a meeting, but he played down speculation that Japan's G-7 partners failed to answer a plea of that nature. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-03-95 \par \par \uc2  0140GMT \par \p