\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slides, Japan seen powerless to act alone. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 8 (Reuter) - The dollar continued its precipitous slide against the yen Wednesday, and Japan appeared powerless to stem the fall without joint action on interest rates from other leading nations. \par \par \uc2  The dollar flirted with new global lows against both the yen and the German mark in afternoon trade despite repeated bursts of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It dropped to around 89.20 yen from its close of 90.35/40 in New York Tuesday, and to about 1.3620 marks from New York's 1.3725/35. \par \par \uc2  The decline, which followed persistent falls here and overseas this week, resumed just after Japanese economic ministers huddled in an emergency meeting and pledged to consider every possible step to cope with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  Economic Planning Agency minister Masahiko Komura told reporters that Japan would consider fresh economic steps to help halt the dollar's drop, but neither he nor other ministers spelled out any specific measures. \par \par \uc2  Some economists have said Japanese firms are better able now to cope with a strong yen than in the past after shifting production overseas and boosting parts imports. \par \par \uc2  But Wednesday ministers and businessmen took a gloomy view of the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market turmoil, which saw the dollar plunge as low as 89.00 yen and 1.3595 marks in New York Tuesday from around 96.30 yen and 1.4555 marks one week ago. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's rapid rise will have a very large impact on Japan's economy and we are taking the situation very seriously," Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "Especially concerning the small and medium-sized firms and industries which have worked so hard, I am at a loss to describe their situation," Takemura added. \par \par \uc2  "With the dollar falling below 90 yen, I think Japanese industry has now entered a crisis," Takeshi Nagano, chairman of the Japan Federation of Employers' Associations (Nikkeiren), told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen tends to hurt Japan's export industries by raising the price of Japanese goods overseas. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise, fears of a stock market slide, and worries about bad debt problems at many Japanese financial institutions have prompted calls for lower interest rates to ease the pain and cap the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2  Economic planning minister Komura said Wednesday that while a decision on cutting Japan's official discount rate was up to the Bank of Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels should be one factor in deciding the timing. But he said the problem was not Japan's alone and urged the United States to also take steps. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's (trade) surplus is on a shrinking trend and it is the United States' deficit which is a problem," Komura said. "There are (steps) we also want the United States to consider." \par \par \uc2  Economists said a move by Japan to cut interest rates in isolation would have little effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  "What we need is something like the Louvre Accord," said Keikichi Honda, economic advisor to the Bank of Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Japan acting alone wouldn't work. America should raise rates and Germany lower rates together with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If that were combined with strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then markets would get the message." \par \par \uc2  In February 1987, the Group of Seven nations agreed their central banks should try to stabilise the dollar to prevent further falls. The United States pledged action to lower its trade deficit, while Japan and West Germany promised lower interest rates and taxes and higher state spending to fire up consumer demand. \par \par \uc2  This time, however, prospects for such joint action are in serious doubt. Many in the market believe Washington wants a lower dollar to make American goods more competitive abroad, and that Germany is only half-hearted about supporting the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Outspoken trade minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said a move by Japan to act alone on interest rates was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Asked if Japan was likely to cut rates, Hashimoto said: "I don't think it is likely. What would happen if only Japan cut interest rates now? What would happen if Japan played its last card?" \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr3800hqc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
