\par\par \b \uc2  Battered dollar edges up, stocks lack direction. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, March 7 (Reuter) - The battered dollar gained slightly against the yen in late Tokyo trade on Tuesday but Asian dealers said the trend was still downward, with regional stock markets lacking direction facing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  Dealers traded the dollar for around 92.78 yen and 1.4028 marks in afternoon trade in Tokyo, slightly weaker than its closing levels in New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  both \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but selling pressure from U.S. funds and other foreign operators was relentless. \par \par \uc2  "Until the (European) Exchange Rate Mechanism troubles are solved, the U.S. demonstrates its stance on the dollar more clearly and the Mexican peso stabilises, the basic trend will not change," said Yumiko Iijima, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MMS International. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had slumped to a record postwar low of 92.45 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks and Nikkei futures ended only a little lower after fluctuating narrowly in a directionless market, with sentiment not too adversely affected. \par \par \uc2  "Expectations that the yen's rise may peak out in the near future seem to be gradually spreading in the market," said Hiroyuki Hamahira, general manager at Nikko Securities. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei share average ended down 85.44 points, or 0.50 percent, at 16,955.28. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong dealers said that although dollar concerns introduced caution, the weakness had been partially discounted. The blue chip Hang Seng Index edged up 44.75 points or 0.55 percent to 8,138.66. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, however, Chit Abada of DBS Securities said foreigners were continuing to unload stocks on fears of another U.S. interest rate rise to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall. \par \par \uc2  Manila's composite index ended 9.139 points lower at 2,447.400 after rising to 2,460.916 by midsession, preliminary figures from the exchange showed. \par \par \uc2  Singapore shares also ended lower on higher rate fears. \par \par \uc2  "Most investors are sidelined and waiting to see if the U.S. Federal Reserve will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check a further slide of the dollar," a broker at a local securities house said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Australia said with all eyes on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets the Australian market lacked any direction. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries index ended 0.4 points lower at 1891.2 after trading in a narrow 8.7-point range. \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakness was putting pressure on cyclical stocks in New Zealand, said Tim Preston of ANZ McCaughan. The NZ-40 share index closed down 1.12 points at 1,961.90. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Taiwan and Seoul ended slightly higher while Bombay stocks were down on local factors. \par \par \uc2  April Brent was quoted at $16.80/83 against the IPE close of $16.86 Monday. April NYMEX crude fell to $18.55 on access in the Asian afternoon versus Monday's settlement of $18.59. \par \par \uc2  Gold bullion was at $378.80/$379.30 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  These are the Asian market highlights: \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG - Shares ended moderately higher in sluggish trade. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index edged up 44.75 points, or 0.55 percent, to 8,138.66. Turnover stood at HK$1.831 billion, compared with Monday's adjusted HK$1.963 billion. \par \par \uc2  "There was obviously some buying interest in the morning but buyers turned cautious later in the day," Teresa Wong, dealing director at DBS Securities, said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said concerns about the dollar made sentiment cautious, although dollar weakness had been partially discounted. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Tokyo stocks and Nikkei futures ended a little lower after fluctuating narrowly in a directionless market. \par \par \uc2  Most investors were sidelined due to a lack of fresh incentives, brokers said, adding that sluggish trade is expected to continue for the rest of the week. \par \par \uc2  "We have no good shares to focus on today and no big activity was seen," said Michio Sugita, head of stock trading at Deutsche Bank Capital Markets (ASIA). \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei share average ended down 85.44 points, or 0.50 percent, at 16,955.28, while the June Nikkei futures closed down 60 points at 17,050 with 21,356 contracts traded. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Singapore shares ended lower in thin trade on concern over a weak dollar and fears of a fresh hike in U.S. interest rates to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Most investors are sidelined and waiting to see if the U.S. Federal Reserve will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check a further slide of the dollar," a broker at a local securities house said. \par \par \uc2  Other brokers said trading was cautious despite generally strong corporate earnings. But for the dollar and trouble at the Barings bank, the market would have done better, they said. Brokers said investors were also still afraid of fund redemption by Barings' asset management \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share Straits Times Industrials lost 28.46 points,s or 1.34 percent, to 2,098.53. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY - Lack of adequate buying support combined with mild profit-booking by corporates weakened Bombay share prices in a day of lacklustre trade, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share Bombay Stock Exchange Index fell 36.56 points, or 1.03 percent, to a provisional close of 3,504.20 while the 100-share composite index declined 8.34 points, or 0.49 percent, to a provisional 1,697.18. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said most speculators had slackened their purchases on news that the Securities and Exchange Board of India would take longer than expected to decide on the recommendations of its panel on a system of forward trading. \par \par \uc2  The watchdog of the country's capital market had set up a panel on February 23 to review the system of forward trading. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MANILA - Philippine shares closed lower despite a firm opening as local bargain-hunters took quick profits in the absence of support from foreign investors, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Manila's composite index ended 9.139 points lower at 2,447.400 after rising to 2,460.916 by midsession, preliminary figures from the exchange showed. \par \par \uc2  Chit Abada of DBS Securities said foreigners continued to unload their holdings on fears of another U.S. interest rate increase after the dollar plunged against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR - Share prices ended softer but off the day's lows in a late wave of speculative buying of second-line stocks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It's mostly election play. We saw some volume after lunch from out of Hong Kong with the funds looking at a mixed bag of stocks," said one institutional dealer with a local brokerage. "I think that they are just in for a quick trade," he added. \par \par \uc2  The key Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange (KLSE) Composite Index ended down 0.65 percent, or 6.17 points lower, at 947.23 on an estimated volume of some 139.9 million shares. Losers outnumbered gainers 287 to 125. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - Stocks closed a session of roller coaster trading higher for the fifth consecutive day after follow-through buying outpaced profit-taking, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The composite stock index added 2.69 points to close at 936.13, off a day high of 941.32. Gainers led losers by 408 to 341 and 167 stocks went limit up. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said buying interest continued in low-priced large-capitalisation shares. \par \par \uc2  "There were lots of investors waiting to buy stocks at lower prices," said Park Key-pyong of Hanyang Securities. "The market will show steady rises this month." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - The Australian share market closed a touch lower in directionless trade after receiving little lead from offshore markets on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries index ended 0.4 points lower at 1891.2 after trading in a narrow 8.7 point range. \par \par \uc2  The share price index futures March contract rose one point to close at a 7.8 point premium to the physical market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said investor focus has shifted to the major international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few sessions and as a result most investors were waiting to see if the U.S. dollar made any more significant moves later on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI - Taiwan share prices closed mixed in thin, narrow-range trade, with minor buying on financials supporting the weak market, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The index inched up 5.82 points, or 0.09 percent, to 6,480.76. Turnover was a small T$30 billion. \par \par \uc2  Most investors have decided to step to the sidelines despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assurance on Monday that it would not change its monetary policy, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "It is just too hard to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not bow to inflationary pressure and maintain its current monetary policy," Lei Pei-yu of Pacific Securities said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON - New Zealand shares ended mixed in thin volume, with strength in Telecom balanced by weakness among the other leaders. \par \par \uc2  The NZ-40 share index closed down 1.12 points at 1,961.90. \par \par \uc2  Telecom advanced four cents to NZ$5.41 but forestry stocks were weaker, with Carter Holt Harvey off three at $3.31 and Fletcher Challenge down one to $3.81. \par \par \uc2  Tim Preston of ANZ McCaughan said cyclical stocks were coming under pressure because of the U.S. dollar's weakness. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr3700lmv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
