\par\par \b \uc2  =Yen Rise/Japan Bond -2-: Money Seen Flowing Into Euroyen \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par 11:23\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Despite several rounds of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the post war low of 92.75 yen early Monday before bouncing back to around 93.40 yen late in the Tokyo day amid Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Gittler at Merrill Lynch says that over the past decade, Japanese investors have lost roughly $400 billion on foreign securities due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations. Some Japanese investors were mulling resuming foreign investment, but now the dollar's sharp depreciation "will keep them at home for a while," he says. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, on a hedged basis, the yield of 10-year U.S. Treasury notes is 100 basis points lower than 10-year Japanese government bonds. On an unhedged basis, the U.S. 10-year notes are around 290 basis points higher than the Japanese counterpart. But given volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where one can lose 3% in a day, such hedging can be meaningless, Gittler says. \par \par \uc2  In addition, a downturn in the Japanese stock market is keeping Japanese investors extra wary about risks, says Katsuzo Sakamoto, the general manager in the bond market intelligence department at Daiwa Securities Co. The slackening stock market erodes hidden assets of Japanese companies and makes them extremely sensitive to risk even as they go through a severe restructuring, Sakamoto says. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's rise is crucial in turning Japanese investors toward yen assets - Euroyen bonds in the case of foreign investment," he says. \par \par \uc2  Sakamoto also expects more capital flow into the yen bond market as Japanese companies increase loan repayments, leaving institutional investors with more cash to invest. He expects the yield on the benchmark 10-year government bonds to fall as low as 4.0%, the lowest level since last June. \par \par \uc2  Monday the yield of the benchmark bond fell below 4.2% for the first time since July 1. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS 03-06-95 \par \par \uc2  0523EST \par \par \uc2  4035 4077 4160 4243 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024dr3600t64\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 17/10/1999\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trga